-- title:
--   Membership in `finPts p` via supersingular orders of g
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and an integer $M \ge 1$ with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$, and a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing the kernel of reduction to $(\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ (hypothesis `hHp`). Let $Pl$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a non-unit in it, whose residue field $\kappa$ has characteristic $p$ and is algebraically closed, and assume the Laurent series `jqModC ℚ` lies in the field `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`. Let $\mathfrak{X}$ be an `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj` (integral model of $X_H(M)$ over $R_p$ together with a curve model `Meta` of the function field $\bar F =$ `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, its comparison `eeta`, and the partial Atkin–Lehner isomorphism $\mathfrak{X}.w$), $\Lambda$ a `LevelData` at $Pl$, and $O$ a `JHNeronObjectAtP` — a smooth separated surjective group scheme $O.g : O.G \to \mathrm{base}\,p$ with relative group law $O.L$ and a bijection $O.\mathrm{pts}$ from $J_H(M) = \mathrm{Pic}^0(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}, \bar F)$ onto the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-sections of $O.g$, compatible with Galois and with Hecke operators. A block of hypotheses, summarised here, pins $O$ down as the relative Picard scheme and $\mathrm{ajbar}$ as the Abel–Jacobi map: $(O.G, O.g, \text{unit section})$, as a `RelativePic0Designation` over $R_p$, represents the rigidified line bundles on $\mathfrak{X}$ with section $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_\infty$ satisfying `FibrewiseAlgEquivZero` (`hD`, and `hDQ` after base change to $\mathbb{Q}$), the generic fibre is separated, $\mathrm{ajQ}$ and the comparison morphism $kQ$ are given with their compatibilities, the Poincaré bundles match under base change (`hpoinc`), $\mathrm{ajQ}$ carries the $\infty$-section to the zero section, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along a point $x$ composed with $\mathrm{ajQ}$ is the line bundle of the Cartier divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the $\infty$-section (`hajQ`), $\mathrm{ajbar}$ is $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{eeta}$ followed by $kQ$, $\mathrm{ajQ}$ and the first projection, $\bar\varepsilon$ is the point above $\varepsilon_\infty$ with $\bar\varepsilon \circ \mathrm{ajbar}$ the identity section, $O.\mathrm{pts}$ is additive for the group law attached to `hD`, and (`hAJ`) for points $x, s$ of `Meta` with $s$ above $\varepsilon_\infty$ the class of the degree-zero divisor $[\,v(x)\,] - [\,v(s)\,]$ is sent by $O.\mathrm{pts}$ to $x$ followed by $\mathrm{ajbar}$. Further, $w_{\mathrm{gen}}$ is a semilinear automorphism of $\bar F$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ inducing $\mathfrak{X}.w$ on places (`hwgen`), $\theta$ is an $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra automorphism of $\bar F$ sending any $f$ whose $q$-expansion is that of a function $u$ of level $\Gamma_{H'}(M/p)$, $H' =$ `infSubgroup p M H hpM`, to one with $q$-expansion $u(q^p)$, and $w_{\mathrm{gen}} =$ `SemilinearAut.ofAlgAut θ`. The conclusion: for every $p$-torsion class $x$ in $\mathrm{Pic}^0(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}, \bar F)$, every degree-zero divisor $D$ with class $x$, every $f \ne 0$ in $\bar F$ with $p \cdot (w_{\mathrm{gen}} \cdot D)(v) = \mathrm{ord}_v f$ at every place $v$ of $\bar F$, and every Laurent series $y$ over $Pl$ whose image under the inclusion is the $q$-expansion of $f$ and whose reduction modulo the maximal ideal is non-zero, and for every $g$ in `qExpFunctionFieldC κ (GammaH (M/p) H')` whose Laurent series is that reduction, one has $x \in O.\mathrm{finPts}\,p$ — the subgroup of $J_H(M)$ generated by the $p$-torsion classes $z$ for which $O.\mathrm{pts}\,z$ satisfies `ExtendsToPlace Pl Λ.σA` — if and only if $p \mid \mathrm{ord}_v g$ for every place $v$ of that $\kappa$-function field lying in `ssPlacesQExp κ (GammaH (M/p) H') p`.
--
--   This is Raynaud's criterion for a $p$-torsion class to lie in the identity component of the special fibre of the Néron model of $J_H(M)$ at a place over $p$, read on the two-component Deligne–Rapoport fibre at level $\Gamma_{H'}(M/p) \cap \Gamma_0(p)$: the vertical part of the divisor of a $p$-th root function is $p$-divisible exactly when the orders at the supersingular points of the restriction $g$ to the $\Sigma^\infty$ component are divisible by $p$. It is used in the construction of a reduced $p$-th root function carrying this finiteness criterion, a step towards the description of the component group entering level lowering.
